I don't remember all the details since a couple of months
back, but I'm looking at some of the 3.4.2 code, and will do
the best I can.

The explanation included with NetHack such as it is is at the bottom of
file:

  <srcRoot>/<nethackVersion>/win/share/tile.doc

mostly starting with the line "tilemap.c (shudder)"; that
parenthetical expression isn't there to be a joke.

What's going on here is that the tile _numbers_ in
objects.txt, other.txt, and monsters.txt are all zero
origined seperately in each file, but still a maintenance
nightmare, so they are *ignored* during compilation, thus
giving possibly invalid clues where to put new tile pixel
color data.  Notice that the three *.txt files are the
original data, but _also_ can be reconstructed to renumber
the tiles consecutively for a release by a loop of
conversion routines.

So how do we match things up compared to the C code so we
get the correct tile for the correct entity?  Via the text
strings following the tile numbers, which may be single
strings, or double strings separated by a "/".

Where are they matched?  In objects.c, monst.c, and rm.h(!!)
of the main source code branch.  If only it were that
simple.  The rm.h strings aren't matched to the other.txt
strings either, since the strings don't _occur_ in rm.h;
simple order of appearance seems to rule; anyone wanting to
modify that dungeon floor and special effects, tile code
for other.txt should just choose suicide instead, it would be
less painful.

Notice that in objects.c it is the array "objects[]", in
monst.c it is the array "mons[]" that contain the index
number and strings data, but in rm.h it is a "simple" set of
defines starting with "S_stone" that contain the index
number data _only_,

So, to insert a new tile/monster, say, you must add an entry
to mons[], add a matching tile definition to monsters.txt,
conditionalize the one in mons[] with an #ifdef/#endif pair,
and (wait for it) add code to tilemap.c in the "win" branch
to correspond to the ifdefs in monst.c!

Based on your error messages, either (1) the text strings in
objects.txt (which in your version, not to throw confusion
after chaos, appears to be objthin.txt instead) occur in a
different order than in objects[] of objects.c, because an
insertion was done in different orders in the two files,
(likely), or else the conditioning code in tilemap.c (or
your version thereof) was (a) forgotten or (b) messed up
(also likely).

You have my heartfelt sympathies, and as much help as I can
give at my larval state of understanding this mess.

